Dataset and Data Collection
Data source: [Yahoo Finance – S&P 500 (^GSPC)](https://finance.yahoo.com/quote/%5EGSPC/history/)


This study utilises historical equity market data representing the S&P 500, a widely recognised benchmark index comprising approximately 500 large-capitalisation companies listed on U.S. stock exchanges. The S&P 500 provides broad coverage of the U.S. equity market across multiple sectors, including technology, healthcare, financials, consumer discretionary, industrials, and energy. Owing to its depth, liquidity, and long historical record, the index is frequently employed in academic research and industry practice as a reference universe for portfolio optimisation, risk analysis, and performance benchmarking.

The dataset is obtained programmatically using the yfinance Python library, an open-source interface that retrieves historical financial data from Yahoo Finance. The use of yfinance enables automated, transparent, and reproducible data collection, which is essential for simulation-based portfolio analysis and continuous reassessment frameworks. This approach also reflects practical constraints faced in academic and small-scale industry environments, where access to proprietary databases such as Bloomberg or CRSP may be limited.

Daily adjusted closing prices are collected for the S&P 500 index (and, where required, selected constituent equities) over a multi-year period. Adjusted close prices incorporate dividends, stock splits, and other corporate actions, ensuring that computed returns accurately reflect investor-realised performance. This adjustment is critical in portfolio optimisation studies, as unadjusted prices can introduce bias into return, volatility, and correlation estimates.

From the adjusted price series, daily logarithmic returns are computed and used as the primary input for all subsequent analysis. Log returns are preferred due to their additive properties over time and their suitability for statistical modelling of financial time series. These return series form the basis for estimating expected returns, variances, and covariance matrices, which are central to mean–variance optimisation, efficient frontier construction, and risk-adjusted performance evaluation.

The S&P 500 dataset is particularly well suited for continuous portfolio reassessment. The index exhibits volatility clustering, time-varying correlations, and regime shifts across market cycles, making it an ideal environment for examining the limitations of static portfolio optimisation and the benefits of rolling, adaptive strategies. The availability of high-frequency daily data further supports rolling-window analysis, allowing the study to dynamically update portfolio risk–return characteristics as new information becomes available.

Overall, the combined use of the S&P 500 as the market universe and yfinance as the data acquisition tool ensures that the dataset is reliable, reproducible, and industry-relevant, while remaining fully accessible through open-source technologies. This choice aligns with the project’s objective of developing a practical, low-cost, and transparent portfolio optimisation and decision-support system that bridges academic theory with real-world application.
